DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmrraaiinnbbooww generates a PPM image that fades from one color  to  another
       to another from left to right, like a rainbow.

       If  you want a vertical or other non-horizontal rainbow, run the output
       through ppnnmmrroottaattee or ppaammfflliipp.

       One use for such a rainbow is to compose it with another image under  a
       transparency  mask in order to add a rainbow area to another image.  In
       fact, you can make rainbow-colored text by using ppbbmmtteexxtt, ppaammccoommpp,  and
       ppppmmrraaiinnbbooww.

       ppggmmrraammpp does a similar thing for grayscale images.

       If  you just want an image containing all the possible colors (for some
       kind of processing; not to look at), see ppaammsseeqq.



AARRGGUUMMEENNTTSS
       _c_o_l_o_r ... is the list of colors, in order from left  to  right,  to  go
       into the rainbow.

       The first color is added again on the right end of the image unless you
       specify the --nnoorreeppeeaatt option.  This means you can concatenate  multiple
       copies (tile, as with ppnnmmttiillee) to make a continuous larger image.

       _c_o_l_o_r  is as described for the argument of the ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(()) library
       routine ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       All options can be abbreviated to their shortest  unique  prefix.   You
       may use two hyphens instead of one to designate an option.  You may use
       either white space or equals signs  between  an  option  name  and  its
       value.



       --wwiiddtthh _n_u_m_b_e_r
              The width in pixels of the output image.

              Default is 600.


       --hheeiigghhtt _n_u_m_b_e_r
              The height in pixels of the output image.

              Default is 8.


       --nnoorreeppeeaatt

              This option makes ppppmmrraaiinnbbooww end the rainbow with the last color
              you specify.  Without this option,  ppppmmrraaiinnbbooww  adds  the  first
              color  you specify to the right end of the rainbow as if you had
              repeated it.


       --ttmmppddiirr
              The directory specification of the directory  ppppmmrraaiinnbbooww  is  to
              use for temporary files.

              Default is the value of the TTMMPPDDIIRR environment variable, or //ttmmpp
              if TTMMPPDDIIRR is not set.

              ppppmmrraaiinnbbooww always creates a directory within this directory  and
              creates all its files within that directory.


       --vveerrbboossee
              Print the "commands" (invocations of other Netpbm programs) that
              ppppmmrraaiinnbbooww uses to create the image.




